name: structs and enums
intro: A struct groups named fields into one value. `typedef` gives it a
intro: short name, and a designated initializer fills it by field, so
intro: order does not matter and anything left out is zero:
intro:
intro:     typedef struct {
intro:         char name[16];
intro:         long score;
intro:     } player;
intro:
intro:     player p = { .score = 40 };
intro:     p = (player){ .score = 41 };   /* compound literal */
intro:
intro: Structs copy by value, so a big one passed to a function is copied
intro: whole. Pass a pointer instead, and `const` when it only reads:
intro:
intro:     static long total(const player *v, size_t n);
intro:     p.score          /* field of a value */
intro:     q->score         /* field through a pointer, same as (*q).score */
intro:
intro: An enum names a set of constants. Switch over one WITHOUT a
intro: `default:` and the compiler warns when you forget a case -- that
intro: warning is worth more than the default:
intro:
intro:     enum op { OP_ADD, OP_SUB };
intro:     switch (op) {
intro:     case OP_ADD: return a + b;
intro:     case OP_SUB: return a - b;
intro:     }
intro:
intro: Each case ends in `return` or `break`; without one, execution
intro: falls into the next case. An array of structs makes a table: a
intro: fixed list of records you loop over with sizeof arr / sizeof arr[0].
---
q: rect.c -- area and perimeter
spec: Takes two arguments, width and height, each a whole number from 0
spec: to 1000. Prints `area A perimeter P` and a newline: `rect 3 4`
spec: prints `area 12 perimeter 14`.
spec:
spec: Keep them in a struct built with a designated initializer, and give
spec: area and perimeter functions that take `const struct rect *`.
spec:
spec: A bad or out-of-range number prints `rect: bad size` to standard
spec: error and exits 1. Any other argument count prints nothing and
spec: exits 2.
file: rect.c
prog: #include <errno.h>
prog: #include <stdbool.h>
prog: #include <stdio.h>
prog: #include <stdlib.h>
prog: 
prog: struct rect {
prog: 	long w;
prog: 	long h;
prog: };
prog: 
prog: static bool parse_size(const char *s, long *out)
prog: {
prog: 	char *end;
prog: 	errno = 0;
prog: 	long v = strtol(s, &end, 10);
prog: 	if (errno != 0 || end == s || *end != '\0' || v < 0 || v > 1000)
prog: 		return false;
prog: 	*out = v;
prog: 	return true;
prog: }
prog: 
prog: static long area(const struct rect *r)
prog: {
prog: 	return r->w * r->h;
prog: }
prog: 
prog: static long perimeter(const struct rect *r)
prog: {
prog: 	return 2 * (r->w + r->h);
prog: }
prog: 
prog: int main(int argc, char *argv[])
prog: {
prog: 	if (argc != 3)
prog: 		return 2;
prog: 
prog: 	long w, h;
prog: 	if (!parse_size(argv[1], &w) || !parse_size(argv[2], &h)) {
prog: 		fprintf(stderr, "rect: bad size\n");
prog: 		return 1;
prog: 	}
prog: 	struct rect r = { .w = w, .h = h };
prog: 	printf("area %ld perimeter %ld\n", area(&r), perimeter(&r));
prog: 	return 0;
prog: }
case: rect 3 4
case: rect 0 5
case: rect 1000 1000
case: rect -1 2 2>&1; echo $?
case: rect 1001 2 2>&1; echo $?
case: rect 3; echo $?
hint: a struct rect holds w and h, the helpers take a pointer and read r->w and r->h
